> In math mode, `\.` is not a dot operator; it’s a **text accent command** (an accent for letters), so LaTeX complains and suggests using `\mathaccent` if you really intend an accent in math. But what you almost certainly want here is just a dot (either punctuation or a multiplication).
> 
> Use one of these instead:
> 
> - If it’s just punctuation (e.g., “= . ImmediateImpact…”), write:
>   ```tex
>   &. ImmediateImpact_{it}
>   ```
> - If it’s a multiplication dot, use:
>   ```tex
>   &\cdot ImmediateImpact_{it}
>   ```
> - If you want a centered dot without semantic meaning (rare, but possible):
>   ```tex
>   &\mathbin{\cdot} ImmediateImpact_{it}
>   ```
> 
> So a likely corrected version of your equation is:
> 
> ```tex
> \begin{align*}
> ModifiedImpact_{it} &= ImmediateImpact_{it}
>   + \sum_{s \le t - K - 1} (1 - \omega)^{t - s} ImmediateImpact_{is} \\
> &\quad - (1 - \omega) \, CummulativeImpact_{i,t-1}
> \end{align*}
> ```
> 
> Note: I also aligned on `=` (more typical for `align`) and removed the stray `\.`.
